[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is a bounded change, so I'll keep the design short and put it here in chat rather than writing a spec. The page,
  its script and its data are already in the repo, and filtering 311 events in the browser fits the settled API
  decisions in NOTES.md.

  The first open question is where the filters go, and that's easier to see than to describe. I've opened a browser tab
  with three layouts at [94m]8;id=neo962;http://localhost:59961/?key=ec5b0f8e021ed66dd55946e7b37cb65a3a2ef65b3f5806ce7108f38cddc226f8\http://localhost:59961/?key=ec5b0f8e021ed66dd55946e7b37cb65a3a2ef65b3f5806ce7108f38cddc226f8[39m]8;;\.
  The tab uses a lot of tokens, so tell me if you'd rather stay in the terminal.

  All three layouts have the same controls: event type, a "Failed sign-ins only" shortcut, and a result count. They
  differ only in placement:

  - [1mA. Filter bar above the table.[0m I recommend this one. The controls are always visible, and the failed sign-in
    shortcut stays in plain view, which matters because every support ticket so far was someone looking for a failed
    sign-in. On a narrow screen the controls stack.
  - [1mB. Side panel beside the table.[0m It takes width away from a table that already has six columns, so the table gets
    cramped well before you reach 320px.
  - [1mC. A "Filter" button that opens a popover, with active filters shown as tags.[0m It's the most compact, but the failed
    sign-in shortcut sits behind a click.

  The mockups are rough sketches. The real version will use the guideline components and tokens.

  Click a layout in the browser or reply here. After that I have a couple of scope questions: which filters you want,
  and the fact that the table is empty with JavaScript off today. Then I'll write up the short design for your yes.

  Also, the companion writes to [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m in the repo, so add that to [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m if it isn't there already.

